docs/agents/gatekeeper-instructions.md:3:Your pile — the body of related work this seat owns — is **taking the git-gatekeeper from built-but-dormant to live.** Every task here shares one specification, one program, and one threat model (the specification's § The credential and enforcement is where that threat model is written down), so each makes the next cheaper. Read [the seat model](agent-seat-model.md) first: it defines the words used here — seat, pile, walked approval, instruction-class, slice, the C-numbers — and this file assumes them.
docs/agents/gatekeeper-instructions.md:5:**Your work is done when** the walked-approval evidence format is designed and slice 6 is built and landed, and everything in the credential work that does not require org-owner powers is prepared and documented, with a written statement to the user of exactly what remains and that it is his to do. Activation itself is not your completion criterion: the final step needs an org owner, so a seat defined as "the gate is live" could never finish. Land what you can, prepare what you cannot, say what is left, write the handoff, stop.
docs/agents/gatekeeper-instructions.md:9:`scripts/git-gatekeeper.py` is **built through slice 5** — every slice of the original five-slice plan — and merged to main (PR #49, 2026-08-12), with a test suite in `scripts/git-gatekeeper-test.py` (169 cases as of 2026-08-13; the count moves as slices land, so run it rather than quoting it). A **sixth slice was added later by ruling** and is not built; that is why "all five slices are built" and "build slice 6" are both true. The gate is **dormant**: no host holds a main-capable credential, so nothing routes through it yet. Agents currently reach main the interim way — commit to a working branch, push, and the user's Mac-side agent reviews and merges (that agent runs on his Mac and is outside the seat model; you reach it by telling the user, not by addressing it directly).
docs/agents/gatekeeper-instructions.md:19:1. **Design the walked-approval evidence format.** Walked approval is the user's approval given item by item through a walk rather than as one yes to a bundle; today it is recorded by quoting his words into `.walk-approved`, which the instruction-file guard consumes for the single write it approves. The open question is what that approval should look like as an artifact a *program* can check at the gate — durable, tied to the specific change, and not forgeable by the agent seeking approval. Undesigned today; slice 6 enforces whatever you design. Which artifacts are gated on it: [nedschorus#31](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/31).
docs/agents/gatekeeper-instructions.md:20:2. **Build slice 6** — the review-evidence check, which enforces that format at the gate. Ruled 2026-08-10 as a prerequisite of activation: the deployed copy of the gatekeeper updates itself from main, so an unapproved edit reaching `scripts/git-gatekeeper.py` on main would become the running gate. Slice 6 closes that against *agents*. It does not close it against the repository's owners, who can bypass branch protection by design — that residual is accepted, not solved, and worth stating plainly if anyone asks whether slice 6 makes the source tamper-proof.
docs/agents/gatekeeper-instructions.md:46:Read the specification and the slice plan, check PR #55's state, and confirm the test suite runs green. Then report to the user where the road stands, and propose starting with the walked-approval evidence format — the only step available to you, since step 2 enforces the format step 1 produces and step 3's GitHub half is his. Say that plainly rather than offering a choice the road's own dependencies have already made.
docs/agents/gatekeeper-instructions.md:48:Do not begin designing the format until he agrees: its shape is his ruling, and the result is instruction-class.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:8:**Implementation status:** branch protection and the account layout are LIVE (applied 2026-07-21; layout amendment ruled 2026-08-09, § The credential and enforcement, not yet applied). `scripts/git-gatekeeper.py` is BUILT through slice 4 of five (2026-08-08/09, per the build order in `docs/issues/3-git-gatekeeper-build-slice-plan.md`; a sixth slice — the review-evidence check — was added by ruling 2026-08-10 as a prerequisite of activating the privileged lane and is NOT yet built, its evidence format undesigned, § Open): the synchronous check-in, the entry checkpoint, and concurrent-check-in integration are running behavior (slice 2's `imports` table subcommand was built, then deleted by ruling 2026-08-10 — the trailer plus `git log --grep` is the view), exercised by a 169-case suite against throwaway repositories (the count moves as slices land; slices 4 and 5 added the lifecycle and audit cases). Slice 4 (the worker lifecycle: `--no-wait` with a detached worker, `status`, `cancel`, the opportunistic expiry sweep) is BUILT 2026-08-12. Slice 5 is BUILT 2026-08-12, completing the five-slice build: the `audit` subcommand answers B3c's three outcomes (protection-ok / protection-wrong with facts naming every differing setting / audit-failed) and rides each session recycle via the fast-handoff writer; the repo git-config pins (user.name, user.email, user.useConfigOnly) are set and test-pinned. The CLAUDE.md workflow line landed the same day, user-walked: the permanent path stated, the dormancy named, the interim lane described — activation day deletes its middle sentence. On this box the live audit answers `audit-failed` honestly: the agent credential cannot read protection settings — the audit reads green only under a credential that can, which arrives with the C-work. The gate is **dormant**: no host yet holds a main-capable credential for it (§ The credential and enforcement).
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:147:- **The Unix-user boundary (C2) — where the enforcement actually lives:** GitHub restricts accounts, not processes, and on one machine every process of one Unix user reads the same credential files. So the main-capable credential is owned by a **dedicated Unix user**, unreadable by agent sessions, and agents invoke the program through a sudoers rule scoped to exactly it. That is the step at which "agents never push" becomes impossible rather than instructed; it also makes the gatekeeper literally an invocable service any agent on the box can call. The sudoers rule names a **root-owned copy of the program outside every checkout** (ruled 2026-08-10) — a rule pointing into a checkout would privilege whatever bytes an agent last wrote there. The copy keeps itself current from main automatically (no manual deploy step; a checked-in fix is live on the next call), which is safe because the gatekeeper's source joins the instruction-file class: changes reach main only with walked-approval evidence, enforced by the review-evidence check (slice 6). Activating the privileged lane therefore waits on slice 6. Working path: `/usr/local/lib/nedschorus-gatekeeper/git-gatekeeper.py`. Staleness of the deployed copy is fail-safe — a stale copy enforces the old contract and never runs bytes an agent planted in place directly — only walked-approved content from main; ownership, not freshness, carries the security guarantee, so a lag in self-update costs availability, never safety. Standing principle, project-wide: a deployed copy of anything keeps itself current from its source automatically rather than relying on a remembered deploy step.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:159:| Review-evidence field + check | No artifact class was gated at founding; the first class was designated 2026-08-04 (instruction-bearing text, nedschorus#31) | Slice 6 — waits on the walked-approval evidence format; scheduled 2026-08-10 as a prerequisite of privileged-lane activation |
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:185:- The walked-approval evidence format (what walked approval looks like as a checkable artifact) — undesigned; slice 6 waits on it.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:189:- Which artifact classes are gated on walked-approval evidence — the first class was designated (boss-ruled 2026-08-04): instruction-bearing text (CLAUDE.md files, skills with their prompt templates, injected system prompts, the wiki), whose check-ins require walked-approval evidence; class definition, procedure, and guards on [nedschorus#31](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/31). The check itself is slice 6, scheduled 2026-08-10 as a prerequisite of privileged-lane activation.

