./docs/wiki/queue/agent-loop-rules-draft.md:5:**1. The fix ladder (user-ruled 2026-07-31, superseding the earlier three-round form).** A failing check gets at most four machine attempts, each by a fresh one-shot fixer — never the same agent twice, never the writer, never the reviewer (rule 5). Attempt 1: a fixer given the design, the code, and the failing evidence. Attempts 2 and 3: new fixers, each also given what earlier fixers tried and why it failed, required to re-examine that evidence before editing. After three failures the frame widens: a two-agent team — one close to the failing part, one carrying the surrounding-system picture — reviews everything with the harness, environment, instructions, and design all treated as suspects, produces a diagnosis, and directs ONE final attempt by a fresh fixer. If that fails, the problem goes to the user with all notes. **Between attempts the tree is reverted to the original failing state** — stacked failed patches are how complexity accretes; a failed attempt's diff rides in the notes, and a later fixer may deliberately re-apply and extend it when its own analysis supports that: the tree stays clean, the knowledge stays in the notes. **Escalation:** work classes run on different model tiers (designs — few tokens, high stakes — on the strongest; code and test writing typically cheaper; evaluation to be decided), so when a loop trips on cheaper-tier work, later fixers and the team may run on stronger models; the headroom is known from the artifact's writer stamp (Gatekeeper-agent trailer — #3 packet B6). Why: each fresh attempt tests a genuinely new theory instead of defending an old one, and the bound (four, then human) exists because non-convergence across independent fresh attempts is itself strong evidence the problem is mis-framed.
./docs/wiki/queue/agent-loop-rules-draft.md:15:**6. Real versus cosmetic changes (user-ruled 2026-07-31).** A **real** change alters behavior in any way, however small — "you forgot this simple case" is always real, and a stream of small real findings on one change is the infeasibility alarm: counted in the ledger, forcing the design question, never triaged away. A **cosmetic** change alters no behavior: renaming, restyling, comments, docs, layout. Cosmetic is not minor — names and comments are how the next agent understands the code, so cosmetic changes can be high-importance — but the two classes are verified oppositely. Real changes need the evidence chain (witness, tests, revert-check per the #21 procedure). Cosmetic changes need evidence of **no behavioral change**: the test suite green, and for comment/layout-only changes a code-object comparison — instruction bytes and structure compared with location metadata excluded (a small comparison tool, not a bare bytecode diff; renames additionally change name tables, so rename-class changes rest on the test suite). Boundary rulings: **changes to prompt files, Markdown instruction files, and CLAUDE.md are never cosmetic** — an instruction file's entire behavior is how LLMs read it, so a wording change is a behavioral change to the system. A code rename is cosmetic unless the code constructs prompts. Review findings carry the classification first (real / cosmetic), then importance within it; cosmetic findings never enter the fix ladder — they queue separately, with their own (sometimes high) value.
